<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      The App Sandbox is deliberately off.

      The app's whole job is to reach the broker's admin socket at
      ~/.config/secretctl/run/admin.sock, and a sandboxed process cannot open a
      Unix socket outside its container. A security-scoped bookmark does not
      help: the path is created by the daemon at runtime. Turning the sandbox on
      would mean either a temporary-exception entitlement for the user's home
      directory — broader than the unsandboxed case, not narrower — or moving
      the socket somewhere the sandbox permits, which would change the daemon's
      contract with the CLI and every SDK.

      Nothing else is needed. The hardened runtime is enabled at signing time and
      the app takes no exceptions to it: no JIT, no unsigned executable memory,
      no library-validation exemption, no Apple Events, no camera, microphone or
      location. Keychain access to its own generic-password item and
      LocalAuthentication both work under the hardened runtime without an
      entitlement, and the app opens no network sockets at all.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
